  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and well above normal conditions will persist \nthrough tomorrow.\n\nDeep southerly flow around a surface high pressure that is exiting \noff the New England coastline this afternoon, combined with the \npassage of a mid level ridge axis is promoting temperatures that are \na good 15 to 20 degrees above normal. \n\nPlenty of sunshine today, but tomorrow will feature a bit more cloud \ncover with the exiting surface high and approach of the upstream mid \nlevel trough. \n\nTemperatures aloft at 850 hPa will be similar/degree lower than \ntoday, and with increased cloud cover, but a milder start to the day \nSunday should increase temperatures back up to around what they are \ntoday.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler air arrives next week, supporting chances for \nsome light rain and/or snow early in the week.\n\nLate Sunday night a cold front will cross our region, this as a \ncoastal low drifts out to sea. As moisture begins to wane along the \ncold front Sunday night and into Monday, rain showers along the \nfront will diminish, with chances for measurable precipitation \nmainly just west of the Genesee Valley. \n\nColder air behind the front will introduce lake clouds and some \nprecipitation east of the Lakes. This strong cold air advection, \nwith 850 hPa temperatures lowering to -5 to -10C at 850 hPa by \nMonday night will create instability for lake showers of rain or \nsnow east of the Lakes, and especially over the higher terrain. \n\nThough the instability will be plenty, moisture behind the front \nwill be lacking, thus much of the lake response will be light, with \nthe best chances for measurable precipitation Monday night when a \nweak uptick in ambient moisture occurs. All precipitation should end \nTuesday morning with increasing amounts of sunshine through the day. \n\nThe cold air advection Monday and Monday night will bring a brisk \nnorthwest flow to our region.",
